lessons.body:
PostgreSQL roles own objects and hold privileges. A role with `LOGIN` is a user; one without is a group. Follow **least privilege**: give every application its own role and grant only the operations it needs, never `SUPERUSER`.

Privileges are granted with `GRANT` and removed with `REVOKE`. A role can only use an object it has been granted, and only inside a schema it can reach (`USAGE` on the schema):

```sql
GRANT USAGE ON SCHEMA reporting TO analyst;
GRANT SELECT ON ALL TABLES IN SCHEMA reporting TO analyst;
REVOKE INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE ON reporting.orders FROM analyst;
```

**Schemas** are namespaces inside a database; the `search_path` decides which one an unqualified name resolves to. Keep untrusted users out of `public` by revoking `CREATE` on it.

**Row-level security (RLS)** filters rows per user. Enable it with `ALTER TABLE tickets ENABLE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;`, then add a policy such as `CREATE POLICY own_rows ON tickets USING (agent_id = current_user_id());` so an agent sees only their rows even though the table grant is shared.

Finally, `pg_hba.conf` decides *who may connect from where and how* - which host addresses, which databases, and which authentication method (`scram-sha-256` preferred). Grant the privilege, then restrict the door.
